  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1136 AM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nWeak high pressure briefly builds in this afternoon as low pressure \nexits to the northeast. Downsloping westerly winds along the Coteau \nand Sisseton Hills will continue to slowly diminish this afternoon \nas the low exits. Upper ridging remains in place through Friday.\n\nAnother low moves off the Rockies and into SD late tonight into \nFriday. South to southeast winds ahead of the low will produce winds \ngusts of 30 to 35 mph from the James Valley east. The southerly flow \nwill usher in a layer of moist air resulting in low stratus and \nperiods of drizzle or sprinkles on Friday. The gusty winds across \nthe east will push grassland fire danger into the high category \nFriday afternoon. However, the drizzle and RH above 50 percent will \nlikely mitigate any fire starts. High temperatures are tricky with \nlow NBM confidence due to competing factors. Good mixing winds and \nthe ridge would lead to highs in the 60s, but the low clouds and \ndrizzle will suppress daytime heating which could lead to some \nlocations topping out closer to 50 degrees. \n\nCaa rushes in behind this system Friday night with H85 temps falling \nfrom +8C to -4C by late Saturday morning as high pressure also \nbuilds in at the sfc. This translates to highs in the 30s, much \ncooler than recent temperatures but closer to climatological \naverages for late November. These cooler temps are forecast through \nnext week and may support flurries or light snow with a system on \nTuesday/Wednesday. Given the strength of the sfc high at that time, \nthough, there would be a lot of dry air to overcome before snow \nactually develops. Will be a system to watch over the coming days as \nwe head into the Thanksgiving travel time, but too much uncertainty \nin outcomes to worry yet.",
